    "q-336876": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336876",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=16$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{x}^{2}-20x+64}{\\sqrt{x}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{[{x}^{2}-20x+64]^{′}}{[\\sqrt{x}-4]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{2x-20}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(16)-20}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{16}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{32-20}{(\\frac{1}{2(4)})} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
