    "q-72325": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72325",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{8{e}^{8x}-5{e}^{5x}-3}{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{8x}-{e}^{5x}-3x}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{8{e}^{8x}-5{e}^{5x}-3}{2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{[8{e}^{8x}-5{e}^{5x}-3]}^{′}}{[2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{64{e}^{8x}-25{e}^{5x}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{64(1)-25(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
