    "q-31270": {
      "dom_id": "step-q31270",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4}=$",
      "worked_solution": "If we plug in $x=2$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15]}^{′}}{{[{x}^{2}-4x+4]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-3)-8}{2x-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{({x}^{2}-3)^{2}-8x+15}{{x}^{2}-4x+4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-3)-8}{2x-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{[4x({x}^{2}-3)-8]}^{′}}{{[2x-4]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{4({x}^{2}-3)+8{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4({2}^{2}-3)+8(2)^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+32}{2} \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
